I'm not sure if this is another bug (I suspect probably not, but still it's unexpected at least), but I've discovered some weird behaviour while writing the test:
```csharp
void Print(InterfaceMapping map)
{
	for (int i = 0; i < map.InterfaceMethods.Length; i++)
	{
		Console.WriteLine($"{map.InterfaceMethods[i]} ({map.InterfaceMethods[i].DeclaringType}) maps to {map.TargetMethods[i]} ({map.TargetMethods[i].DeclaringType})");
	}
	Console.WriteLine();
}

Print(typeof(C2).GetInterfaceMap(typeof(I2)));
Print(typeof(C2).GetInterfaceMap(typeof(I1)));
Print(typeof(C1).GetInterfaceMap(typeof(I1)));

public interface I1
{
	static abstract void A();
	void B();
}

public class C1 : I1
{
	static void I1.A() { }
	void I1.B() { }
}

public interface I2 : I1
{
	static void I1.A() { }
	void I1.B() { }
}

public class C2 : I2
{
	static void I1.A() { }
	void I1.B() { }
}
```

->

```
Void I1.B() (I2) maps to Void I1.B() (I2)

Void B() (I1) maps to Void I1.B() (C2)
Void A() (I1) maps to Void I1.A() (C2)

Void B() (I1) maps to Void I1.B() (C1)
Void A() (I1) maps to Void I1.A() (C1)
```

the behaviour of the first set seems quite odd, is this what is expected? And if so, why?
Specifically, I would have expected any of the 3: 1. no entries, 2. entries for both `A` and `B` default implementations in `I2`, or 3. entries for the actual implementations in `C2`.

Option 1 or 2 probably makes the most sense, but it seems like static methods do it differently to instance methods for some reason. Option 1 (no entries) probably makes the most sense overall for both of them from a consumer point of view, since these methods are not supposed to be overriden themselves, but instead provide a default implementation for `I1`, but one could argue that from an IL point of view option 2 makes more sense for the instance method, since it's marked as virtual (but the instance method is also marked as final...).


EDIT:
actually, the behaviour is even weirder than I thought:
```csharp
void Print(InterfaceMapping map)
{
	for (int i = 0; i < map.InterfaceMethods.Length; i++)
	{
		Console.WriteLine($"{map.InterfaceMethods[i]?.ToString() ?? "null"} ({map.InterfaceMethods[i]?.DeclaringType?.ToString() ?? "null"}) maps to {map.TargetMethods[i]?.ToString() ?? "null"} ({map.TargetMethods[i]?.DeclaringType?.ToString() ?? "null"})");
	}
	Console.WriteLine();
}

Print(typeof(C3).GetInterfaceMap(typeof(I3)));
Print(typeof(C3).GetInterfaceMap(typeof(I2)));
Print(typeof(C3).GetInterfaceMap(typeof(I1)));
Print(typeof(C2).GetInterfaceMap(typeof(I2)));
Print(typeof(C2).GetInterfaceMap(typeof(I1)));
Print(typeof(C1).GetInterfaceMap(typeof(I1)));

public interface I1
{
	static abstract void A();
	void B();
}

public class C1 : I1
{
	static void I1.A() { }
	void I1.B() { }
}

public interface I2 : I1
{
	static void I1.A() { }
	void I1.B() { }
}

public class C2 : I2
{
	static void I1.A() { }
	void I1.B() { }
}

public interface I3 : I2
{
	static abstract void I1.A();
	abstract void I1.B();
}

public class C3 : I3
{
	static void I1.A() { }
	void I1.B() { }
}
```

->

```
Void I1.B() (I3) maps to null (null)
Void I1.A() (I3) maps to null (null)

Void I1.B() (I2) maps to Void I1.B() (I2)

Void B() (I1) maps to Void I1.B() (C3)
Void A() (I1) maps to Void I1.A() (C3)

Void I1.B() (I2) maps to Void I1.B() (I2)

Void B() (I1) maps to Void I1.B() (C2)
Void A() (I1) maps to Void I1.A() (C2)

Void B() (I1) maps to Void I1.B() (C1)
Void A() (I1) maps to Void I1.A() (C1)
```

This seems even less ideal, since the arrays on `GetInterfaceMap` do not even have their element marked as nullable.
While this probably(?) is the behaviour the runtime should produce, it's certainly not what a user would probably expect here.

I've found another instance of the original bug which wasn't fixed by recent changes:
```csharp
var x1 = typeof(I2<string>).GetMethod("I1<S>.G", (BindingFlags)(-1));
var x2 = typeof(C2<string>).GetInterfaceMap(typeof(I1<string>)).TargetMethods.Single((x) => x.Name == "I1<S>.G");
Console.WriteLine(x1 == x2);
Console.WriteLine(x1.MakeGenericMethod(typeof(int)).ContainsGenericParameters);
Console.WriteLine(x2.MakeGenericMethod(typeof(int)).ContainsGenericParameters);

interface I1<S>
{
	static abstract void G<T>();
}

interface I2<S> : I1<S>
{
	static void I1<S>.G<T>() { }
}

class C2<S> : I2<S>
{
}
```

->

```
False
False
Unhandled exception. System.InvalidOperationException: Void I1<S>.G[T]() is not a GenericMethodDefinition. MakeGenericMethod may only be called on a method for which MethodBase.IsGenericMethodDefinition is true.
   at System.Reflection.RuntimeMethodInfo.MakeGenericMethod(Type[] methodInstantiation)
```

I found it with the tests I'm writing.
